<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Granville Sharp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1735">1735</a>–<a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Granville Sharp was an English scholar and campaigner, one of the first British activists for the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. He brought legal cases on behalf of enslaved Africans, including that of James <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> in 1772. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-for-the-relief-of-the-black-poor">Committee for the Relief of the Black Poor</a> he organised the settlement of the Province of Freedom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> in 1787, and he later helped found the Society for Effecting the Abolition of the Slave Trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/granville-sharp.jpg" alt="Granville Sharp" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G. Dance · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Granville_Sharp_(Hoare_memoire).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sharp was born in Durham, the son of Thomas Sharp, Archdeacon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, and Judith Wheler, and a grandson of John Sharp, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>. The family's funds for education were nearly spent, so he was taught mainly at home, and at fifteen he was apprenticed to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> linen-draper. To argue with his fellow apprentices he taught himself Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. In 1757 he became a clerk in the Ordnance Office at the Tower of London, a post that left him time for study.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1765, at his brother William's surgery, Sharp met Jonathan Strong, a young enslaved man from Barbados whom his master, the lawyer David Lisle, had beaten close to blindness and cast into the street. The brothers paid for his treatment at Barts Hospital. In 1767 Lisle sold Strong to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> planter, James Kerr, and had him seized, but Sharp took the case to the Lord Mayor, who freed him. In 1769 Sharp published the first tract in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><p>In 1783 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaudah-equiano">Olaudah Equiano</a> told him of the Zong, whose crew had thrown an estimated 132 enslaved people overboard so that the owners could claim insurance; the court refused the claim, but Sharp's attempt to bring a prosecution for murder never got off the ground. In 1787 he was unanimously chosen chairman of the new Society for Effecting the Abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade</a>, though he never took the chair at meetings and failed to persuade the committee to target <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> itself.</p><p>Sharp sympathised with the American colonists and resigned from the Ordnance Office in 1776 rather than supply the war against them. He also championed parliamentary reform, the legislative independence of Ireland and an end to the impressment of sailors. A classicist and biblical scholar, he set out the Granville Sharp Rule on the Greek article and was a founder of the British and Foreign Bible Society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sharp lived to see the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abolition-of-the-atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade Act</a> receive royal assent on 25 March 1807 and died on 6 July 1813. He was buried at All Saints' Church, Fulham, and is commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, on the Clarkson Memorial in Wisbech and in the names of Granville Town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and the free village of Granville in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. In 2007 Royal Mail put him on a stamp marking the 200th anniversary of the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Granville Sharp” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1772, in a case brought with the help of <b>Granville Sharp</b>, Lord Mansfield ruled that James Somerset, an enslaved man in England, could not be forcibly removed to be sold in Jamaica, a decision widely, if wrongly, believed to have ended slavery in England.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The Province of Freedom grew out of the campaign by <b>Granville Sharp</b> and the Committee for the Relief of the Black Poor to resettle impoverished Black people in London, many of them Loyalists from the American War of Independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-freedom">Province of Freedom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Durham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.78,-1.57&amp;year=1785" aria-label="Durham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4516 289 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Granville Sharp"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4966 559h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4966 559h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.78,-1.57&amp;year=1785">What was here in 1785? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
